<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Urgell</h1><p class="st-meta">798 CE – 1413 CE (approx.) · capital Balaguer</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The longest-lasting Catalan county, ruled by the Ermengol counts from Balaguer, whose bishops became co-princes of Andorra.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Urgell, in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segre">Segre</a> valley, was taken by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a> around 798 and organised as a county of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marca-hispanica">Marca Hispanica</a>, often joined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy">Wilfred the Hairy</a> ruled it from 870. In 992 it passed to Ermengol I, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borrell-ii-of-barcelona">Borrell II of Barcelona</a>, founding a dynasty that lasted over two centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts of Urgell, nearly all named Ermengol, pushed south against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a> in 1105, making it their capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-seu-durgell">La Seu d'Urgell</a> held great influence, and an agreement of 1278 with the counts of Foix made them co-lords of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/andorra">Andorra</a>. Later the county passed to a branch of the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-barcelona">House of Barcelona</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-urgell">James II</a> claimed the throne of Aragon in 1410, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compromise-of-caspe">Compromise of Caspe</a> of 1412 chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-aragon">Ferdinand of Antequera</a>. James rebelled, was besieged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a> in 1413 and imprisoned, and the county was confiscated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Urgell remains co-prince of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/andorra">Andorra</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-urgell.jpg" alt="County of Urgell" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_County_of_Urgell.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>798 CE – 1413 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Balaguer</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ermengol I, James II of Urgell</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Catalan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>, <a href="/countries/andorra">Andorra</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Comtat d'Urgell</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1106&amp;focus=county-of-urgell" aria-label="County of Urgell on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4715 671 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Urgell lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5035 927L5037 922M5035 927L5139 672M5035 927L4984 945"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5037 922h0M5139 672h0M4984 945h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5035 927h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5035 927h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1106&amp;focus=county-of-urgell">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

